WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.424
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 1: There’s a Lesson in That.

2
00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.861
Unit 1 asks what stories are for.

3
00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:15.722
You read “The Camel and the Sparrow”, a fable in which a
boastful camel learns that the smallest helper found the water,

4
00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:18.307
and “The Silence After the Rumble”,

5
00:00:18.387 --> 00:00:24.805
in which Amal sweeps the steps of a courthouse
in a town rebuilding after an earthquake.

6
00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:33.149
Alongside them you meet twenty-five new words and the
adjective and preposition pairs that make feelings precise,

7
00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:36.768
such as proud of, anxious about and grateful for.

8
00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:41.863
The unit ends with a fable of your
own, planned, drafted and revised.

9
00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:48.066
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:48.146 --> 00:00:56.078
By the end of this unit, you will be able to read a
fable closely and state its moral in your own words.

11
00:00:56.158 --> 00:01:04.322
You will also identify the features of fables and
folklore, including animal characters and a stated moral.

12
00:01:04.402 --> 00:01:10.948
You will also use adjective and preposition
pairs accurately in speech and in writing.

13
00:01:11.028 --> 00:01:13.799
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:19.864
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
coal, pouch, encounter, regions, misty, anxiety.

15
00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:25.091
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:25.171 --> 00:01:29.156
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:32.202
The reading sequence includes What Makes a Fable?

18
00:01:32.282 --> 00:01:34.067
and Folklore Around the World.

19
00:01:34.147 --> 00:01:34.937
Read actively.

20
00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:39.227
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

21
00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:43.267
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

22
00:01:43.347 --> 00:01:47.673
The first language focus is What
Are Adjectives + Prepositions?

23
00:01:47.753 --> 00:01:53.826
An adjective describes a noun or a pronoun
by naming a quality, a feeling or an opinion.

24
00:01:53.906 --> 00:02:01.378
After a verb, many adjectives cannot stand alone, so
they reach forward to a preposition to finish the idea.

25
00:02:01.458 --> 00:02:09.000
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

26
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.843
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Talk About Stories That Teach.

27
00:02:13.923 --> 00:02:18.288
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

28
00:02:18.368 --> 00:02:21.671
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

29
00:02:21.751 --> 00:02:25.187
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

30
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:26.840
You are ready to begin.

31
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:33.955
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

32
00:02:34.035 --> 00:02:37.404
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

33
00:02:37.484 --> 00:02:45.525
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
